      }, {
        "text": " Dynamics}\nThe simulation executed in discrete time steps of one minute. In simulation time, each time step took approximately $"
      }, {
        "text": "0.1$ seconds. This step size was chosen because all reviewed publications regarding metabolism kinetics expressed rates in minutes.\n\n"
      }, {
        "text": "In each time step, the organ graph was traversed using a breadth-first search. In the first time step, initial peanut macronutrients"
      }, {
        "text": " (\\autoref{sec:micronutrient-inputs}) were input directly to the mouth. Then, the mouth executed kinetics for"
      }, {
        "text": " all contained metabolic agents and placed destination organs for all output edges (in this case, only the stomach) into a queue. The"
      }, {
        "text": " next organ-node was removed from the queue, its metabolic agents were executed, and destination organs were added to the queue. This"
      }, {
        "text": " process iterated until all organs had been visited.\n\nFor all future time steps, the process repeated starting with the mouth. However"
      }, {
        "text": ", nutrients were only input into the mouth in the first time step, modeling a single mouthful of peanuts ingested all at once"
      }, {
        "text": ".\n\n\\section{Explanation of Mathematical Models}\nFrom reviewed kinetics publications, first-order and pseudo-first-"
      }, {
        "text": "order \\autocite{libretexts_pseudo_2013} exponential models were common. Others presented a"
      }, {
        "text": " form of the Michaelis-Menten equation, a popular representation for enzymatic kinetics \\autocite{sriniv"
      }, {
        "text": "asan_guide_2022}. Ten such models were employed within the simulation. Most models were adopted assuming that"
      }, {
        "text": " the reaction would never be limited by the availability of the enzyme, but only by the concentration of the substrate.\n\nNot all"
      }, {
        "text": " reactions throughout the simulation used a tested model from the literature. Instead, some included naive approaches to facilitate the demonstration of the end"
      }, {
        "text": "-to-end pipeline. \\autoref{tab:metabolizer-kinetics} summarizes the substrates, enzymes,"
      }, {
        "text": " and kinetics equation type for each metabolic process.\n\nAll reaction models were solved numerically using a first-order, ordinary differential equations solver ("
      }, {
