        "text": "}\n\n\\subsection{Mouth}\nThe mouth was assumed to fully empty its contents into the stomach within a single"
      }, {
        "text": ", one-minute time step. The presence of the esophagus was omitted for this model.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amylase}\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\label{sec:amylase}\nFor simplicity, starch is treated as $100\\%$ amylose,"
      }, {
        "text": " which is converted to maltose by amylolysis. However, real starch metabolism would also include a pathway to catabolize"
      }, {
        "text": " amylopectin \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Real amylolysis would also produce"
      }, {
        "text": " maltotriose and $\\alpha$-limit dextrins \\autocite{roberts_mechanism_1960"
      }, {
        "text": ", butterworth_enzyme_2022}. \n\nThe mouth contains salivary amylase, and other types of"
      }, {
        "text": " amylase are included in organs below. All amylase reactions were modeled using the same kinetics for pancreatic amylase: a first-order"
      }, {
        "text": " exponential with a rate constant of $0.03$ (starch half-life of $23$ minutes) \\aut"
      }, {
        "text": "ocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Because a model of amylase action specifically on peanuts was"
      }, {
        "text": " not available, the results for beans were selected, as both are legumes with similar macronutrients \\autocite{toomer_"
      }, {
        "text": "nutritional_2018}.\n\n\\subsection{Stomach}\n\\subsubsection{Gastric Amylase"
      }, {
        "text": "}\nThe same model presented in \\autoref{sec:amylase} acted within the stomach on remaining starch passed from the"
      }, {
        "text": " mouth.\n\n\\subsubsection{Pepsin}\nAs no protein catabolism was considered within the mouth, all proteins ($\\"
      }, {
        "text": "alpha$-arachins) were passed directly to the stomach. Pepsin converted arachin into arachin polypeptides. As a"
      }, {
        "text": " proxy, a model fitting the hydrolysis of milk casein by pepsin was used, with a first-order exponential rate constant of $"
      }, {
        "text": "0.08$ (protein half-life of approximately $9$ minutes) \\autocite{yang_"
      }, {
        "text": "kinetics_2022}.\n\nEffects from pepsin denaturation were not considered, though other models have noted increased denaturation at pH"
      }, {
        "text": " levels over $5.0$ \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}, which may"
      }, {
        "text": " occur after meal ingestion \\autocite{gastric_acid_regulation_2006}. Our model further assumed that"
      }, {
        "text": " pepsin was already active upon food ingestion, ignoring the preliminary activation of pepsinogen by hydrochloric acid.\n\n\\subsubsection{St"
      }, {
        "text": "omach Emptying Regulator}\nAfter nutrients in the stomach have undergone enzymatic reactions and formed into acid chyme, they are"
      }, {
        "text": " emptied into the duodenum. This step is tightly controlled via the pyloric sphincter at a potential rate of $2$--$4$ kcal"
      }, {
        "text": "/min \\autocite{kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nOur simulation used a power exponential"
      }, {
        "text": " function reviewed by Kong et al. (2008). This model included a power constant to account for an initial"
      }, {
        "text": " lag period in the emptying of solid foods. With this model, $50\\%$ of solids were expected to be emptied"
      }, {
        "text": " in approximately $94$ minutes, and completely emptied in $2$ or more hours \\autocite{sie"
      }, {
        "text": "gel_biphasic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "subsection{Small Intestine}\n\\subsubsection{Pancreatic Amylase}\nThe model for pancreatic amylase matched"
      }, {
        "text": " the details of \\autoref{sec:amylase}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Trypsin}\nTrypsin was the only"
      }, {
        "text": " enzyme considered for converting polypeptides into small peptide chains. Our model based the catabolic rate on findings by Schwert and Eisenberg (1"
      }, {
        "text": "949). Experimental results followed a zero-order (linear) hydrolysis rate of $1.1 \\times "
      }, {
        "text": "10^{-3}$ mmol/min of polypeptides \\autocite{schwert_kinetics_194"
      }, {
        "text": "9}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Lipase}\nOnly pancreatic lipase was considered for the conversion of triglycerides to mono-glycer"
      }, {
        "text": "ides. The selected model presented distinct first-order reactions for the hydrolysis of triolein to 1,2-dio"
      }, {
        "text": "lein, and then 1,2-diolein to 2-monolein. Diglycerides were cat"
      }, {
        "text": "abolized nearly three times faster than triglycerides \\autocite{lykidis_kinetics_1995}."
      }, {
        "text": "\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine Lining}\n\\subsubsection{Maltose Hydrolysis}\nMaltose was"
      }, {
        "text": " hydrolyzed to glucose by maltase. A Michaelis-Menten model was selected, with parameters derived from experiments with rat jej"
      }, {
        "text": "unums \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_2021}, adjusted for typical human small intestine"
      }, {
        "text": " length \\autocite{fish_physiology_2025} and fluid volume \\autocite{schiller_"
      }, {
        "text": "intestinal_2005}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Peptidase}\nPeptidase catalyzes the reaction converting peptide chains into"
      }, {
        "text": " individual amino acids (assumed to be glutamic acid). For demonstration, this is assumed to be first-order,"
      }, {
        "text": " catabolizing $20\\%$ of existing peptide chains during each time step.\n\n\\subsubsection{Mixed Micelle Formation"
      }, {
        "text": "}\nMonoglycerides and free fatty acids form mixed micelles with bile salts. A naive model was implemented in which"
      }, {
        "text": " $1$ mole of monolein, $2$ moles of oleic acid, and $1$ mole of bile"
      }, {
        "text": " coalesce to form $1$ mole of micelle, consuming $50\\%$ of the limiting reactant.\n\n\\subsection{"
      }, {
        "text": "Liver}\n\\subsubsection{Bile Salt Production}\nBile salt was modeled to be injected directly from the liver"
      }, {
        "text": " to the small intestine. Our modeled liver produced and transported $1$ mmol of bile salt per time step.\n\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "subsection{Pancreas}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Production}\n\\label{sec:insulin-production}\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Insulin production relied on a model using both plasma glucose and plasma amino acids as regulators. The model assigned linear coefficients to"
      }, {
        "text": " current levels of glucose, amino acids, and insulin to determine the secretion rate \\autocite{morettini_mathematical"
      }, {
        "text": "_2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Bloodstream}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Degradation}\nThe"
      }, {
        "text": " model accounted for insulin degradation within the blood via first-order feedback. Assuming a steady-state average insulin level $I_b"
      }, {
        "text": "$ of $7.5$ $\\mu\\text{U/mL}$ and a rate coefficient $k_I$ of $"
      }, {
        "text": "0.14$, insulin was expected to naturally degrade to its base level \\autocite{morettini_"
      }, {
        "text": "mathematical_2022}. The specific kinetics of the insulin-degrading enzyme (half-life of $"
      }, {
        "text": "4$--$6$ minutes) were not explicitly modeled \\autocite{vitamins_hormones_ide_20"
      }, {
        "text": "09}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glucose Uptake Regulator}\nGlucose uptake across cell membranes was implemented as a rate limiter between"
      }, {
        "text": " the blood and cytosol. The maximum uptake per unit time was modeled as a Hill function \\autocite{goutelle"
      }, {
        "text": "_hill_2008} of insulin concentration \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_20"
      }, {
        "text": "16}. \n\n\\subsection{Within Cells}\nCytosol, mitochondrial matrix, and inner membrane areas were modeled as a"
      }, {
        "text": " single cell volume representing a total count of $3.0 \\times 10^{13}$ cells participating in energy generation \\"
      }, {
        "text": "autocite{hatton_human_2023}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glycolysis - Cytos"
      }, {
        "text": "ol}\nGlycolysis treated ATP levels as effectively unlimited. The rate of change for intermediates (G6P, F"
      }, {
        "text": "6P, F16BP, 3PG, PEP, and pyruvate) was modeled with Michaelis-Ment"
      }, {
        "text": "en and Hill kinetics \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}. \\autoref{tab:"
      }, {
        "text": "glycolysis-dependencies} shows simplified metabolite dependencies.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption"
      }, {
