      }, {
        "text": " \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Small Intestine Lining \u0026 Maltose hydrolysis, peptidase,"
      }, {
        "text": " mixed micelle formation \u0026 Maltose, glucose, peptide chain, glutamic acid, bile salt, micelle \u0026 MAG"
      }, {
        "text": ", oleic acid \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Enterocytes \u0026 Chylomicron production \u0026 Micelle"
      }, {
        "text": ", chylomicron \u0026 -- \\\\     \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Pancreas \u0026 Insulin production \u0026 Insulin"
      }, {
        "text": " \u0026 -- \\\\           \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Liver \u0026 Bile salt production \u0026 Bile salt \u0026 -- \\\\"
      }, {
        "text": "                                \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Blood \u0026 Lipoprotein lipase, glucose uptake regulator \u0026 Glucose, insulin"
      }, {
        "text": ", chylomicrons, oleic acid, glycerol, glutamic acid \u0026 Chylomicron remnant \\\\\n  \\"
      }, {
        "text": "hline                                                                        \n  Cytosol \u0026 Glycolysis, acyl-CoA synthase \u0026 Glucose, pyruvate, ole"
      }, {
        "text": "ic acid, fatty acyl-CoA \u0026 G6P, F6P, F16BP, 3PG"
      }, {
        "text": ", PEP \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Mitochondrial Matrix \u0026 $\\alpha$-Alanine amino transferase"
      }, {
        "text": ", TCA cycle, fatty acid oxidation \u0026 Pyruvate, glutamic acid, fatty acyl-CoA, alanine, $\\alpha"
      }, {
        "text": "$-ketoglutarate \u0026 GTP, acetyl-CoA \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Mitochondrial"
      }, {
        "text": " Inner Membrane \u0026 Electron transport chain \u0026 NADH, FADH$_2$, ATP \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                              "
      }, {
        "text": "          \n  \\end{tabularx} \n      \\begin{tablenotes}\n      \\small\n"
      }, {
        "text": "      \\item Note: The term \"organ\" was applied to describe a general area, or set of areas, within"
      }, {
        "text": " the metabolic pipeline. Not all items categorized here, such as blood or the cytosol, meet the typical definition of an organ"
      }, {
        "text": ".\n    \\end{tablenotes}\n  \\end{threeparttable}\n\\end{table"
      }, {
        "text": "}                                                           \n\nThe organs in \\autoref{tab:organ-metabolizers} were organized in a directional,"
      }, {
        "text": " pseudo-acyclic graph structure. In each simulation time step, an organ receives input metabolites from its predecessor (for example"
      }, {
        "text": ", the small intestine takes maltose from the stomach). After processing, the organ outputs relevant substrates downstream (i.e"
      }, {
        "text": "., the small intestine outputs glucose to the intestinal lining). This graph structure is demonstrated in \\autoref{fig:graph-"
      }, {
        "text": "view}.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\linewidth]{graph"
      }, {
        "text": "-view.png}\n    \\caption{Directional graph view of flow of metabolites between organs, including key regulators"
      }, {
        "text": " and metabolites passed through the pipeline}\n    \\label{fig:graph-view}\n\\end{figure}"
      }, {
        "text": "\n\nThis graph-based pipeline is not fully acyclic because the blood acts as an input, output, and regulatory aid"
      }, {
        "text": " for other organs. In this model, for example, the pancreas outputs insulin to the bloodstream. However, it also must"
      }, {
        "text": " access the quantities (inputs) of glucose and amino acids within the blood to produce the correct amount of insulin \\autoc"
      }, {
        "text": "ite{morettini_mathematical_2022}.\n\nEach listed organ was treated as a fully isolated"
      }, {
        "text": " node which only receives inputs as discrete events. No continuous passage of substrates occurred in the model.\n\n\\subsection{Declar"
      }, {
        "text": "ative Model Organization}\nWithin the code, the model was designed to have a declarative structure, such that functioning code also"
      }, {
        "text": " logically outlined the organization of the human body. Each organ was represented as an object that contained its metabolic agents and rate-"
      }, {
        "text": "limiting functions (\\autoref{fig:code-a}). Each metabolic agent contained a list of its substrates and regulators (\\autoref"
      }, {
        "text": "{fig:code-b}). To create the graph structure, organs were placed within nodes, alongside edges including output metabolites"
      }, {
        "text": " and their destinations (\\autoref{fig:code-c}). \n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n     "
      }, {
        "text": "\\centering\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n         \\centering\n         "
      }, {
        "text": "\\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{organ.png}\n         \\caption{organelle with metabolic processes}\n         "
      }, {
