        "text": "     \n     \\caption{Example declarative instantiations of physical metabolic organization within code, using the mitochondria (a),"
      }, {
        "text": " the action of $\\alpha$-alanine aminotransferase (b), and the passage of NADH and FADH$_2$ to"
      }, {
        "text": " the inner mitochondrial membrane (c)}\n     \\label{fig:three_images_layout}\n\\end{"
      }, {
        "text": "figure}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Dynamics}\nThe simulation executed in discrete time steps of one minute. In simulation time"
      }, {
        "text": ", each time step took approximately 0.1 seconds. This step size was chosen because all reviewed publications of metabolism kinetics"
      }, {
        "text": " expressed rates in minutes.\n\nIn each time step, the above organ graph was traversed in a breadth-first search."
      }, {
        "text": " In the first time step, initial peanut macronutrients (\\autoref{sec:micronutrient-inputs}) were input directly to"
      }, {
        "text": " the mouth. Then, the mouth executed kinetics for all contained metabolic agents (none) and placed destination organs for all output"
      }, {
        "text": " edges (only the stomach) into a queue. The next organ-node was removed from the queue, its metabolic agents"
      }, {
        "text": " executed, and destination organs added to the queue. This process iterated until all organs had been visited.\n\nFor all future"
      }, {
        "text": " time steps, the above process repeated, starting with the mouth. However, nutrients were only input into the mouth in the"
      }, {
        "text": " first time step, modeling a single mouthful of peanuts ingested all at once.\n\n\\section{Explanation of Mathematical Models}"
      }, {
        "text": "\nFrom reviewed kinetics publications, first-order and pseudo-first-order \\autocite{libretexts_pseudo"
      }, {
        "text": "_2013} exponential models were common. Others presented a form of the Michaelis-Menten equation,"
      }, {
        "text": " a popular representation for enzymatic kinetics \\autocite{srinivasan_guide_2022}. Ten"
      }, {
        "text": " such models were employed within the simulation. Most models were adopted assuming that the reaction would never be limited by the availability of"
      }, {
        "text": " the enzyme, but only the concentration of the substrate.\n\nNot all reactions throughout the simulation used a tested model from the"
      }, {
        "text": " literature. Instead, some included naive approaches to facilitate demonstration of the end-to-end pipeline. \\autoref{tab"
      }, {
        "text": ":metabolizer-kinetics} summarizes the substrates, enzymes, and kinetics equation type for each metabolic process. Below"
      }, {
        "text": ", the formulation of each process in the table is detailed.\n\nAll reaction models were solved numerically using a first-order"
      }, {
        "text": ", ordinary differential equations solver (see \\nameref{sec:software-design}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h"
      }, {
