      }, {
        "text": "\\end{tabularx}                                                                                                               \n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Mouth}\n"
      }, {
        "text": "The mouth was assumed to fully empty its contents into the stomach within a single, one-minute time step. The presence"
      }, {
        "text": " of the esophagus between the mouth and stomach was omitted.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amylase}\n\\label{sec"
      }, {
        "text": ":amylase}\nFor simplicity, starch is treated as 100 percent amylose, which is converted to"
      }, {
        "text": " maltose by amylolysis. However, real starch metabolism would also include a pathway to catabolize contained amylopect"
      }, {
        "text": "in \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Real amylolysis would also produce maltotri"
      }, {
        "text": "ose and $\\alpha$-limit dextrins \\autocite{roberts_mechanism_1960, butter"
      }, {
        "text": "worth_enzyme_2022}. \n\nThe mouth contains salivary amylase, and other types of amylase"
      }, {
        "text": " are included in organs below. All amylase reactions were modeled after the same kinetics for pancreatic amylase, a first-"
      }, {
        "text": "order exponential with a rate constant of 0.03 (or starch half-life of 23 minutes)"
      }, {
        "text": " \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Because a model of amylase action specifically on"
      }, {
        "text": " peanuts was not available, the results provided by Butterworth et al. (2012) for beans were selected."
      }, {
        "text": " Peanuts and beans are in the same legume family and share similarities in macronutrients \\autocite{toomer_"
      }, {
        "text": "nutritional_2018}.\n\n\\subsection{Stomach}\n\\subsubsection{Gastric Amylase"
      }, {
        "text": "}\nThe same model presented in \\autoref{sec:amylase} acted within the stomach on remaining starch passed from"
      }, {
        "text": " the mouth.\n\n\\subsubsection{Pepsin}\nAs no protein catabolism was considered within the mouth, all proteins"
      }, {
        "text": " ($\\alpha$-arachins) were passed directly to the stomach. Pepsin converted arachin into arachin polypeptides. As"
      }, {
        "text": " a proxy for this reaction, a model fitting the hydrolysis of milk casein by pepsin was used, with a first-"
      }, {
        "text": "order exponential rate constant of 0.08 (protein half-life of approximately 9 minutes) \\autoc"
      }, {
        "text": "ite{yang_kinetics_2022}.\n\nEffects on the reaction from pepsin denaturation were not considered"
      }, {
        "text": ". However, other models have considered this effect, particularly for increased denaturation at pH levels over 5.0 \\aut"
      }, {
        "text": "ocite{yang_kinetics_2022}. Stomach acidity may approach this level after ingestion of a meal"
      }, {
        "text": " \\autocite{gastric_acid_regulation_2006}.\n\nOur model further assumed that pep"
      }, {
        "text": "sin was already active in the stomach upon food ingestion, ignoring preliminary activation of pepsinogen to pepsin by hydrochloric acid"
      }, {
        "text": " within the stomach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Stomach Emptying Regulator}\nAfter nutrients in the stomach have undergone enzymatic reactions"
      }, {
        "text": " and formed into acid chyme, they are emptied into the duodenum of the small intestine. This step is tightly controlled via"
      }, {
        "text": " the pyloric sphincter at a potential rate of 2--4 kilocalories per minute \\autocite{kong"
      }, {
        "text": "_disintegration_2008}.\n\nOur simulation used a power exponential function reviewed by Kong et al. ("
      }, {
        "text": "2008). This model resembled other first-order exponential models discussed but included a power constant to account for an"
      }, {
        "text": " initial lag period in the emptying of solid foods. With this model, 50 percent of solids were expected to be"
      }, {
        "text": " emptied in approximately 94 minutes, and completely emptied in 2 or more hours \\autocite{siegel"
      }, {
        "text": "_biphasic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nWhile this"
      }, {
        "text": " model did not differentiate between distinct macronutrients, as chyme passes to the small intestine in real metabolism, a striation"
      }, {
        "text": " of chyme would be expected, with emulsified fats on top. Our simulation did not account for such separation and instead"
      }, {
        "text": " assumed that all nutrients were distributed and emptied in equal proportion.\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine}\n\\subsubsection{"
      }, {
        "text": "Pancreatic Amylase}\nThe model for pancreatic amylase matched the details of \\autoref{sec:amylase"
      }, {
        "text": "}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Trypsin}\nTo simplify, trypsin was the only enzyme considered in converting polypeptides within the small"
      }, {
        "text": " intestine into small peptide chains. However, a more robust model should also consider the presence of chymotrypsin and carbox"
      }, {
        "text": "ypeptidase in this step.\n\nOur model based the catabolic rate of polypeptides on findings by Schwert and Eisenberg"
      }, {
        "text": " (1949) of the action of trypsin on benzoyl-L-argininamide. While the authors"
      }, {
        "text": " noted that the reaction theoretically requires higher-order modeling due to varying levels of competitive inhibition depending on substrate concentration, experimental results"
      }, {
        "text": " followed a zero-order (linear) hydrolysis rate of $1.1 \\times 10^{-3}$ mill"
      }, {
        "text": "imoles per minute of polypeptides \\autocite{schwert_kinetics_1949}. This same"
      }, {
        "text": " zero-order model was selected for simulation.\n\n\\subsubsection{Lipase}\nLipases which catalyze the conversion of"
      }, {
        "text": " triglycerides to di- and mono-glycerides are present in the mouth, stomach, small intestine, and intestinal lining."
      }, {
        "text": " Because most lipolysis occurs within the small intestine after emulsification via bile salts (not available to the stomach or mouth),"
      }, {
        "text": " only lipase within the small intestine (pancreatic lipase) was considered.\n\nThe selected model of pancreatic lipase presented distinct first"
      }, {
        "text": "-order reactions for the hydrolysis of triolein to 1,2-diolein, and then 1,"
      }, {
        "text": "2-diolein to 2-monolein. Each reaction was dependent on the initial concentration of triolein"
      }, {
        "text": " and rate constants for both substeps. Diglycerides were catabolized nearly three times faster than triglycerides \\autocite{lyk"
      }, {
        "text": "idis_kinetics_1995}.\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine Lining}\n\\subsubsection{M"
      }, {
        "text": "altose Hydrolysis}\nMaltose resulting from amylolysis earlier in the organ pipeline was hydrolyzed to glucose. This"
      }, {
        "text": " reaction is catalyzed by maltase. A Michaelis-Menten equation model was selected, whose numerical parameters were derived from"
      }, {
        "text": " experiments with rat small jejunums \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_2021}."
      }, {
        "text": " \n\nTo apply this model to humans, a typical human small intestine length \\autocite{fish_physiology_"
      }, {
        "text": "2025} and fed intestinal fluid volume \\autocite{schiller_intestinal_2005"
      }, {
        "text": "} replaced the values used for rats.\n\n\\subsubsection{Peptidase}\nPeptidase catalyzes the hydrolysis reaction converting peptide"
      }, {
        "text": " chains into individual amino acids (all assumed to be glutamic acid). \n\nThe rate for this step is not sourced"
      }, {
        "text": ". For demonstration, it is assumed to be first-order, catabolizing 20 percent of existing small peptide"
      }, {
        "text": " chains during each time step.\n\n\\subsubsection{Mixed Micelle Formation}\nTo enter the enterocytes, monogly"
      }, {
        "text": "cerides and free fatty acids within the small intestinal lining first form emulsified mixed micelles with bile salts. \n\nK"
      }, {
        "text": "inetics for this process were not sourced. A naive model was implemented in which a single mole of monolein, two"
      }, {
        "text": " moles of oleic acid, and one mole of bile coalesce to form a mole of micelle. The lowest-quantity"
      }, {
        "text": " substrate was selected as the limiting reactant in each time step, and the reaction consumed 50 percent of the limiting reactant"
      }, {
        "text": " alongside corresponding proportions of both other substrates.\n\n\\subsubsection{Chylomicron Production}\nAfter passing into the enterocytes"
      }, {
        "text": " via mixed micelles, glycerides and fatty acids form into chylomicrons, which are water-soluble and hence may"
      }, {
        "text": " enter the bloodstream.\n\nEnterocytes were modeled as a distinct organ in the simulation to emphasize this step. For simplicity,"
      }, {
        "text": " 50 percent of micelles from the intestinal lining per time step were assumed to eventually produce chylomicrons, with"
      }, {
        "text": " all intermediate steps omitted.  \n\n\\subsection{Liver}\n\\subsubsection{Bile Salt Production}\nBile"
      }, {
        "text": " salt was modeled to be injected directly to the small intestine from the liver. However, in reality, bile would first be"
      }, {
        "text": " stored within the gallbladder and later delivered via the bile duct. Our modeled liver produced and transported 1 mmol of bile salt"
      }, {
        "text": " per time step.\n\n\\subsection{Pancreas}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Production}\n\\label{sec"
      }, {
        "text": ":insulin-production}\nInsulin production by the pancreas, and secretion to the bloodstream, relied on a model with"
      }, {
        "text": " both plasma glucose and plasma amino acids as regulators. To determine the needed level of insulin in a time step, the model"
      }, {
        "text": " assigned linear coefficients to current levels of glucose, amino acids, and insulin. Those coefficients were applied to the differences between current"
      }, {
        "text": " levels and assumed base levels, such that a higher change from base levels corresponded to a higher change in insulin \\autoc"
      }, {
        "text": "ite{morettini_mathematical_2022}.\n\nWe adopted this model by communicating calculated values of"
      }, {
        "text": " glucose and glutamic acid within the simulated bloodstream to the pancreas node, which were used as parameters in the provided linear equation"
      }, {
        "text": ".\n\n\\subsection{Bloodstream}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Degradation}\nThe same model used in \\"
      }, {
        "text": "autoref{sec:insulin-production} accounted for insulin degradation within the blood via first-order feedback. Assuming a steady"
      }, {
        "text": "-state average insulin level $I_b$ of approximately 7.5 $\\mu$U/mL and a"
      }, {
        "text": " rate coefficient $k_I$ of 0.14, insulin in the next time step was denatured by"
      }, {
        "text": " \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}:\n\n\\[Insulin(t)"
      }, {
        "text": " -\u003d k_I * (Insulin(t - 1) - I_b)\\]\n\nIn this manner"
      }, {
        "text": ", insulin was expected to naturally degrade to its base level in steady state. The specific kinetics of the insulin-degrading"
      }, {
        "text": " enzyme, which contribute to an expected insulin half-life of 4--6 minutes \\autocite{vitamins_hormones"
      }, {
        "text": "_ide_2009}, were not included in this approach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glucose Uptake Regulator}"
      }, {
        "text": "\nInsulin levels in the blood affect the rate at which glucose is absorbed into cells. Passage of glucose from the blood"
      }, {
        "text": " across the cell membrane was implemented as a one-way edge between the blood and cytosol nodes. Glucose uptake regulation was implemented"
      }, {
        "text": " as a rate limiter for this edge.\n\nEstimated parameters were selected from a Michaelis-Menten equation of glucose uptake"
      }, {
        "text": ", fit to glucose and insulin readings of human adults. This equation modeled the maximum glucose uptake per unit time as a Hill"
      }, {
        "text": " function \\autocite{goutelle_hill_2008} of insulin concentration at the active site"
      }, {
        "text": " \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_2016}. \n\n\\subsection{Within Cells}"
      }, {
        "text": "\nDistinct areas of cells, including the cytosol, mitochondrial matrix, and inner mitochondrial membrane, were modeled as though the volume"
      }, {
        "text": " of participating cells of the body were combined into a single cell, with equal distribution of nutrients from the blood to all areas"
      }, {
        "text": ". This volume was visualized as single, pseudo-organ nodes for each area. Types of cells were not differentiated, and"
      }, {
        "text": " a total count of $3.00 \\times 10^{13}$ cells participating in glycolysis and energy"
      }, {
        "text": " generation was assumed \\autocite{hatton_human_2023}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glycol"
      }, {
        "text": "ysis - Cytosol}\nGlucose which enters the cell cytosol undergoes glycolysis with the final output of pyruvate. The simulation"
      }, {
        "text": " assumed only aerobic conditions and, hence, that zero pyruvate was ever later reduced to lactic acid. \n\nFurther, glycolysis"
      }, {
        "text": " and other catabolic processes within the cells treated adenosine triphosphate (ATP) levels as effectively unlimited. Their availability was not considered"
      }, {
        "text": " for substrate inputs nor outputs (excluding the ETC).\n\nThe selected model considered seven different intermediate substrates, in order of appearance"
      }, {
        "text": " in glycolysis: glucose, glucose-6-phosphate (G6P), fructose-6-phosphate (F6P"
      }, {
        "text": "), fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (F16BP), 3-phosphoglycerate (3"
      }, {
        "text": "PG), phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP), and pyruvate. The rate of change of each was modeled with Michaelis-"
      }, {
        "text": "Menten and Hill kinetics as a function of other intermediates \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2"
      }, {
        "text": "014}.\n\n\\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} shows highly simplified relationships between each of these"
      }, {
        "text": " substrates. While the table omits feedback effects on particular glycolytic enzymes, expected examples of feed-forward and backward regulation"
      }, {
        "text": " are present. For example, F16BP increases the later production of PEP from 3PG by allosterically"
      }, {
        "text": " activating pyruvate kinase \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}. As a backwards-"
      }, {
        "text": "feedback example, G6P\u0027s inhibition of hexokinase is encoded in \\autoref{tab:glycolysis-"
      }, {
        "text": "dependencies} as \"self-regulation.\"\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Gly"
      }, {
        "text": "colysis ODE Metabolite Dependencies}\n\\label{tab:glycolysis-dependencies}\n\\renewcommand{\\array"
      }, {
        "text": "stretch}{1.3} \n\\begin{NiceTabular}{llc}[\n    code-before \u003d"
      }, {
        "text": " {\n        \\rowcolors{2}{gray!10}{white}[cols\u003d{2-3}]\n    "
      }, {
        "text": "}\n]\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metabolite} \u0026 \\textbf{Dependency} \u0026 \\textbf{"
      }, {
        "text": "Effect} \\\\\n\\midrule\nGLC (Intracellular Glucose) \u0026 GLC \u0026 $\\pm$ (self-limiting"
      }, {
        "text": ") \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{3-1}{G6P} \n    \u0026 GLC \u0026 +"
      }, {
        "text": " \\\\\n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 +  "
      }, {
        "text": "\\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{2-1}{F6P} \n    \u0026 G6P \u0026"
      }, {
        "text": " + \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{2"
      }, {
        "text": "-1}{F16BP} \n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16"
      }, {
        "text": "BP \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{3-1}{3PG} "
      }, {
        "text": "\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\"
      }, {
        "text": "\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{3-1}{PEP} \n    "
      }, {
        "text": "\u0026 3PG \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F1"
      }, {
        "text": "6BP \u0026 $-$  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{2-1}{Pyruvate} \n    \u0026"
      }, {
        "text": " PEP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{Nice"
      }, {
        "text": "Tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nOur simulation lacked key glycolysis regulation steps due to the omission of non-substrate regulators."
      }, {
        "text": " In particular, no enzymes were regulated by the presence of energy-yielding molecules such as ATP, adenosine diphosphate (ADP"
      }, {
        "text": "), and adenosine monophosphate (AMP).\n\n\\subsubsection{Fatty Acid Oxidation -- Mitochondrial Matrix}\nThe reactions below"
      }, {
        "text": " within the mitochondrial matrix are implemented naively in that inputs from previous steps were assumed to be converted directly into energy-yielding"
      }, {
        "text": " molecules. However, net quantities of energy-yielding molecules output were expected to be realistic. To retain a pool of cycle"
      }, {
        "text": " inputs for visualization, only 75 percent were processed in each time step for fatty acid oxidation, conversion of amino acids"
      }, {
        "text": " to alanine, and the TCA cycle.\n\n1 mole of Fatty acyl-CoA produced a mole of acetyl-CoA based"
      }, {
        "text": " on the length of the fatty acid carbon chain:\n\\[Acetyl-CoA \u003d N_C / 2 - "
      }, {
        "text": "1\\]\nin addition to 1 mole NADH and 1 mole FADH$_2$ \\autocite{"
      }, {
        "text": "brunton2025lecture}. As oleic acid has a carbon length of 18 \\autocite"
      }, {
        "text": "{settaluri_peanuts_2012, pubchem_oleic_nodate}, each step produced "
      }, {
        "text": "8 moles of Acetyl-CoA per mole of oleic acid.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amino Acid Catabolism - Mitochondrial"
      }, {
        "text": " Matrix}\nGlutamic acid and pyruvate react to form $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate and alanine when catalyzed by"
      }, {
        "text": " $\\alpha$-alanine aminotransferase \\autocite{lenartowicz_significance_1988}. $\\"
      }, {
        "text": "alpha$-ketoglutarate can then enter the TCA cycle (\\autoref{sec:tca}). Our model assumed that"
      }, {
        "text": " glutamic acid would always be the limiting substrate of this reaction, such that, in each time step, 75"
      }, {
        "text": " percent of contained glutamic acid was consumed along with an equal number of moles of pyruvate.\n\n\\subsubsection{TCA"
      }, {
        "text": " Cycle - Mitochondrial Matrix}\n\\label{sec:tca}\nEach mole of acetyl-CoA and pyruvate produced"
      }, {
        "text": " 3 moles NADH, 1 mole FADH$_2$, and 1 mole guanosine triphosphate (GTP). $\\"
      }, {
        "text": "alpha$-ketoglutarate produced 2 moles NADH, 1 mole FADH$_2$, and 1 mole"
      }, {
        "text": " GTP \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. The mitochondrial matrix included two processes with the same input"
      }, {
        "text": " substrate—both amino acid catabolism and the TCA cycle required pyruvate. Distribution of pyruvate to these two processes was implemented via"
      }, {
        "text": " a simple, ordered list: first, all available glutamate was converted; then, all remaining pyruvate was passed to the numerical"
      }, {
        "text": " solver for the TCA.\n\n\\subsubsection{ETC - Mitochondrial Inner Membrane}\nThe ETC model assumed that 2 moles"
      }, {
        "text": " ATP were produced per mole NADH and 1.5 moles per mole FADH$_2$. The maximum production rate was"
      }, {
        "text": " 100 ATP molecules per minute per ATP-synthase \\autocite{brunton2025"
      }, {
        "text": "lecture}. Total production considered the total number of active synthases as a product of synthases per mitochondria, mitochondria per cell"
      }, {
        "text": ", and total cells (\\nameref{sec:physical-constants}).\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\subsection{"
      }, {
        "text": "Strengths}\nThis simulation presented a unified set of researched reaction kinetics. While such kinetics of major metabolic steps are accessible"
      }, {
        "text": " in the literature, relatively little has been published combining many together to demonstrate larger-scale effects in the body.\n\nThis"
      }, {
        "text": " work closely coupled demonstrative code with an intuitive organization of metabolism and scientific sources. Based on a limited search, such design"
      }, {
        "text": " and prioritization of code accessibility appear to be novel in biochemical kinetics research.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\nAt the time"
      }, {
        "text": " of writing, this simulation is a prototype intended to demonstrate knowledge at an introductory graduate level. It included steps which are known"
      }, {
        "text": " to be based on incorrect assumptions and omitted important steps, particularly in regulation. As the software had not been part of a"
      }, {
        "text": " rigorous review, the code likely contained further defects.\n\nModeling for this simulation was not based on a thorough literature review."
      }, {
        "text": " High-level model flow was taken from course lecture content \\autocite{brunton2025lecture},"
      }, {
        "text": " and more specific sources for individual reactions were selected on their strengths as easily accessible and relevant parameterized models. Projected accuracy within the"
      }, {
        "text": " overall metabolic pipeline was not the highest priority.\n\n\\subsection{Future work}\nThe possible improvements noted above are strong"
      }, {
        "text": " candidates to improve the robustness of the presented simulation. To remove overly strong assumptions, a literature review should be conducted to better"
      }, {
        "text": " select models for each interaction. More recently researched models should be added, including realistic models of the transfer of metabolites between organs"
      }, {
        "text": " and organelles (not considered in the current simulation).\n\nThe code should be reviewed and improved for maintainability by multiple investigators"
      }, {
        "text": ". It can then be considered a tool to continue mapping intuitive physical body structure to functioning code which demonstrates the current state of"
      }, {
        "text": " the literature. Such a tool will be useful to unify metabolism research and identify gaps.\n\nNo anabolic processes have yet been"
      }, {
        "text": " included, such as the transportation of glycerol from triglyceride catabolism to the liver for either gluconeogenesis or fatty acid reconstruction"
      }, {
        "text": ". Addition of these processes would augment the demonstrative capability of this simulation as a learning tool. Further, the developed codebase"
      }, {
        "text": " is equipped to support such new transports and processes with minimal time investment.\n\n\n\\section{Appendix I: Physical Constants}"
      }, {
